"For each cluster, estimate how far the shift can move before the mean square offset rises by its own value divided by the number of stars in the fit; that change in shift is roughly the standard error of the fitted shift. Turn it into a percentage uncertainty in distance and compare it with how far off the cluster’s distance was. The spread \\(\\sigma_M\\) of M among stars of one type limits a spectroscopic distance: a spread of \\(\\sigma_M\\) in M is a fractional spread of about \\(\\frac{\\ln 10}{5}\\sigma_M = 0.46\\,\\sigma_M\\) in d. For Before the lab {#}, find the uncertainty of the spectroscopic distance in percent, and compare it with the Uncertainty (%) column for the ten stars. Give the fitted shift and distance of each cluster with its uncertainty.",
